<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Casa de la Vall</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Casa de la Vall is a stone manor house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andorra-la-vella">Andorra la Vella</a>, built in 1580 as the residence of the Busquets family and acquired in 1702 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-council-of-andorra">Consell General</a> as its seat. It housed the Andorran parliament and court of justice until 2011, when the Consell General moved to a new building nearby, and it remains a symbol of Andorran self-government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casa-de-la-vall.jpg" alt="Casa de la Vall" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zinneke · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_de_la_Vall_2015-10.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The house stands in the historic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andorra-la-vella">Andorra la Vella</a> on a rocky spur, the roc de la Vall, overlooking the valley. Before the Council bought it, it was known as Casa Busquets; the keystone of its entrance arch bears the family's tree emblem, the date 1580 and an abbreviated form of the name Antoni Busquets. It follows the type of a large Pyrenean manor house, an almost square block with thick walls, and has machicolations, corner turrets with loopholes and a defensive tower over the old royal road into the town.</p><p>Above the door is the quartered coat of arms of Andorra, with the motto Virtus unita fortior, and beside it an older relief signed Escoter me fecit, regarded as the oldest surviving representation of the Andorran arms. Inside, a hall carries 16th-century paintings of the Passion, and the council chamber holds a chapel of Saint Ermengol and the Cupboard of the Seven Keys, the Council's old archive, which opens only when the keys of every parish are present.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Council of the Land had met in church porches and cemeteries before it agreed on 19 December 1701 to buy the house for 1,650 lliures. The house also held the criminal court, the Tribunal de Corts, and from the 18th century a prison. Because sittings could last several days, councillors ate in the kitchen and slept in the attic, later divided into one room for each parish. On 5 April 1933 young Andorrans besieged the Council there, an episode remembered as winning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a>, and in 1991 and 1992 the attic hosted the commission that drafted the Constitution of 1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the early 1960s the Catalan architect Joaquim Ros de Ramis remodelled the house, stripping the render to bare stone and adding a second corner turret. The architect Enric Dilmé and the historian Quim Valera later called the exposed stone a historical fake, which nonetheless became a model for Andorran building. Dilmé restored the interior in 2025, and the house reopened on 22 December 2025 with the Saint Thomas session. It is protected as a bé d'interès cultural and appears on the Andorran 1 euro coin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Casa de la Vall” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1419 the co-princes allowed the creation of the Consell de la Terra, an assembly of parish representatives that developed into the Consell General, which from 1702 met in the <b>Casa de la Vall</b> in Andorra la Vella.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
